WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:03.150
SPEAKER 1: Apigee consists
of the following two

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.440
primary components--

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.660
Apigee Services,
which are the APIs

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.680
that you use to create, manage,
and deploy your API proxies,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.530
also known as the Apigee APIs.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.660
Next is the Apigee
Runtime, which

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.720
is a set of containerized
services in a Kubernetes

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.090
cluster that Google maintains.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.730
All your API traffic
passes through it.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.970
Additionally, Apigee uses
other Google Cloud Services,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.200
such as identity management,
logging, analytics, metrics,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.940
and project management function.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.530
And then there are
backend services

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.270
used by your apps to provide
runtime access to data.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.770
To get started with setting up
your environment with Apigee,

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.800
you can choose to create
an evaluation or a paid

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.830
environment.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.790
In this video, I will share
the differences between the two

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.790
and how to start off with
the evaluation version, which

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.570
is also known as a
test or eval org.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.450
Before diving into
Apigee, you need

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.330
to have a Google Cloud
billing account created

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:06.290
or have management access to
that Google Cloud project.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.410
With that in place, you can
create an evaluation org.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.120
And when you are
ready, you can purchase

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.150
a paid version that requires
a contract with Google.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.790
Now the key differences
are the following.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.070
An evaluation org
is time-limited

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.750
and lacks the scalability and
flexibility of production orgs.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.300
Although an eval
org is free of cost,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.250
you do need to set up a billing
account in your Google Cloud

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:29.750
project.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.210
But Google covers
your cost, and the org

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.430
is deleted after 60 days.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.090
And finally, when using the
UI to provision your eval org,

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.670
you will have fewer
options than if you did so

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.230
via the command line.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.390
For this video, we
will walk through how

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.250
to create an evaluation org.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.790
It is very similar to that
of creating a paid org.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.410
And in this video's
description, I

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.490
have linked several
helpful resources.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.730
So let's dive in.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.800
Creating an Apigee
evaluation org

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.430
is straightforward and can
be done using the UI, wizard,

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.850
or command line.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.410
There's also a GitHub script
for the command line simplifying

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.000
that approach.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.540
Let's use the wizard, since this
is the simplest and quickest,

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.730
which is in the Docs.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.920
First, I enter the
name of my project,

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.350
or I can choose to
create a new one.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.810
Note that the wizard
detects that it is not

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.630
a project with paid
entitlements and can only

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.850
be used for evaluation.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.890
Next, click the Edit icon next
to APIs and choose Enable APIs.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.470
This takes a couple of
minutes to complete.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.980
A check will display next
to the Enable APIs step,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.160
and the Networking
step will be available.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.410
Click Edit next to Networking.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.040
The Set Up Networking
pane is then displayed.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.240
Select a network from the
authorized network dropdown

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.560
list.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.230
For most eval orgs,
you select the network

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.930
that Google Cloud created when
you created your Google Cloud

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.930
project, called
Default. However,

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:02.810
if you have a custom
network and peering range,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.000
you want to use select
it from the list.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.980
For this example, I will
choose Default, Connect.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.250
Next, I will select the option
Automatically Allocate IP

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:13.770
Range.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.400
But if you already have one or
wish to create one yourself,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.130
you can select the desired
option from the dropdown.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.130
The IP range is for peering
between your Google Cloud

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.350
project and Apigee,
and the process also

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.940
takes a couple of
minutes to complete.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.430
Now that networking
is configured,

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:29.960
we can provision the org.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.980
Select the analytics and
runtime regions closest to you.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.380
Click Provision.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.280
This can take anywhere
from 20 to 30 minutes.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.510
Once the organization
has been provisioned,

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.450
you will get an
email notification.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.060
Your Apigee evaluation
is ready to use.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.360
Let's go back to the
wizard and move on

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.580
to configuring access routing.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.620
This allows you to
configure access

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:58.880
to your Apigee endpoint, either
internally or externally.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.730
Internal limits access
to the Apigee endpoint

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:04.250
to VMs in your GCP project.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.030
This is the internal
load balancer.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.720
External allows access to your
APIs over the public internet

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.080
through the configuration
of an HTTP global load

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.260
balancer with a public
static IP address.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.120
Creating an external
load balancer

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.110
can incur fees for up to $20
to $25 in your project, which

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.210
can be covered by the
free project credits

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.370
I linked again in
the description.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:31.490
For this example, I will choose
the external option, which is

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:34.070
called Enable Internet Access.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.890
You now have the option
to configure the hostname.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.380
If you have access
to a DNS registry

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:42.710
and have an existing domain,
you can enter your own hostname

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:46.370
and then create a new DNS record
for your hostname pointing

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.530
to the static IP address.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.110
If you do not have
a hostname, you

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.100
can use a wild card DNS
service, such as nip.io,

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.350
which is suggested.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:00.110
It will give you a hostname
using the external IP

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.890
address of the global load
balancer with a domain suffix

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:05.540
nip.io.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.520
With this approach,
Google automatically

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.010
provisions and manages
the SSL certificates

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.820
for the global load balancer.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.910
This is the easiest
and quickest path

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.180
to getting your org operational.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.450
And here's what that would
look like when finished.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.090
Alternatively, you can
specify your own hostname

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.770
from a domain you own.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:26.870
I have entered
mine, which becomes

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.750
the hostname for my APIs.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:33.230
This also shows us the runtime
location and the internal load

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.510
balancer for the
Apigee endpoint.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.430
It's now asking for a subnet.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.770
And I can choose an option
in the dropdown, which

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.140
in this case is
called Default and is

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.330
in the same region
as the runtime.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:48.110
I then have the option to use
my own SSL certificates and key.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.210
Or I can skip this
by leaving it blank

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.040
and have Google create and
manage them automatically.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.920
This is the easiest.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.350
Then select Set Access.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.620
Note, this step can take
about 10 minutes to complete.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.890
In this step, you can see the
progress of each component

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.810
being created.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.580
Upon completion,
choose Continue.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.300
Here we can see that
Apigee has provided the IP

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.000
address of the global
load balancer, which I now

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.130
use to create an entry
in my DNS configuration.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.160
So let's go ahead and do that.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.770
In this example, our domain
is hosted on Google Domains.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:25.630
Here I create a
new custom record

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:30.400
using the prefix of Type
A, and add the IP address,

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.690
then click Save.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.660
This will take about 10 minutes
to propagate on Google Domains

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.540
but may vary depending
on your domain host.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.030
Once that happens,
I can choose Launch

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.830
to see the "Hello, Guest!"

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.170
response from the
Hello World proxy

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:47.130
that Apigee created for us.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:47.630
Great.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.850
Now we can go to
the Apigee console

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:52.340
and begin creating
our own proxies, which

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.460
we have a video for you to
reference as a next step.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.320
And there you have it,
a quick walkthrough

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.290
on how to create your first
evaluation org in Apigee

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.660
using the wizard interface.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.643
And friends, if you found
this episode helpful,

00:07:04.643 --> 00:07:06.560
you can click Like or
subscribe to our channel

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:07.970
for more content like this.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:08.570
Cheers.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:11.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

